  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nAfter an initial band of rain that swept from west to east across \nthe area this morning, most of the local area has remained dry \nthrough mid afternoon due to the influence of a mid level dry slot. \nVorticity advection associated with mid/upper level low across \ncentral IL is beginning to push eastward.  An instability axis of \n1000-1500 J/kg SBCAPE is continuing to nudge northward in modest \nsoutherly flow regime downstream if this IL disturbance and should \nallow for continued shower and storm development across northern \nIndiana over the next several hours. A pocket of stronger effective \nshear on the order of 30-35 knots may end up just skirting far \nsouth/southeast portions of the forecast area across far NE IN/NW OH \nearly this evening which is also coincident with pocket of 100-150 0-\n1km SRH. Some isolated wind threat is possible in a brief window \nlate this afternoon/early evening far south/southeast, but currently \nexpecting greater potential of isolated-scattered severe storms to \nremain across central IN.\n\nThe potential of heavy rainfall will likely be the primary impact \nlocally. An impressive low level theta-e ridge that shifted into the \narea this morning is acquiring more of a west-northwest to \neast-southeast orientation in response to induced cyclonic flow\nfrom upstream IL mid/upper level low. This should create \nfavorable deep moisture profiles and efficient warm rain \nprocesses tonight into early Sunday. Showers and embedded \nstorms should fill in across northern IN through early evening \nespecially along and south of US 24, with an eventual pivot in \norientation of most favored rain shower axis to SW-NE oriented \nfrom west central IN to as possibly as far east as NW Ohio late \nevening/early overnight as deformation forcing begins to sharpen\nup. This setup should provide favorable low level moisture \nconvergence for a relatively extended duration tonight into \nearly Sunday morning. \n\nEarlier today, some highly efficient rainfall accumulation was noted \nacross NW Indiana with the leading band of showers, with some \nlocations picking up a quick 1 to 2 inches. Earlier today, did go \nahead and issue a Flood Watch for potential of flash flooding/areal \nflooding for all of IN counties from 21Z this afternoon to 21Z \nFriday, although greatest potential of heavy rain is roughly \nfrom early this evening through mid morning Sunday. Given the \nabove evolution, the far north/northwest portions of the Flood \nWatch may be at a bit lower confidence level for flooding rains \ngiven increasing signals that the favored deformation forcing \naxis may reside from southwest portions of the forecast area \nthrough far NE IN. Did exclude NW OH counties as part of the \ninitial Flood Watch issuance, but an eastward extension of the \nwatch may be needed at some point. One mitigating factor to\nflood potential tonight could be relatively dry antecedent\nconditions, but if higher rainfall rates are able to anchor in\ndeformation region tonight, this will still likely lead to some\nflooding issues for a portion of the area.\n\nStrength of deformation should tend to weaken Sunday \nafternoon/evening as responsible mid/upper level trough begins to \nbecome attenuated and more sheared. Conditions should dry out from \nNE to SE later Sunday afternoon into Sunday evening with a quieter \nweather pattern through Tuesday as a longwave mid/upper level ridge \nshifts across Ontario and the western Great Lakes. An active series \nof Pacific waves across south central Canada/Dakotas will dampen \nthis ridge middle of the week with local area possibly in a favored \ntheta-e gradient region for periodic waves in west-northwest upper \nflow for additional shower/storm chances through much of the long \nterm period.",
